  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 AM EST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nHigh pressure over the central Appalachians promotes southerly \nsurface flow on its western edge. Combined with unseasonable upper-\nlevel ridging (579dm!) and our mild stretch of weather continues with \nhighs in the mid-60s both today and Saturday. Through November 18, \nthe average temperature this month of about 47.5 degrees ranks as \nthe 17-18th warmest (South Bend, Fort Wayne respectively) on record. \nThis warmth fades away with a cold front arriving Sunday. High \npressure moves offshore in response a trough emerging over the \nCentral Plains. A parent low tracks northeast across the Hudson Bay \nwith an elongated cold front in its wake. Moisture return into the \nfront is acceptable, with a hint of Gulf moisture slipping poleward \nalong a subtropical ridge. This results in high-confidence POPs \n(around 80%). Yet, forward motion is swift and upper-level forcing \nis displaced across central WI resulting in small rain amounts \n(0.10\" or less). Hardly a washout Sunday with rain arriving from \nwest to east starting in the early afternoon. With this afternoon \ntiming, northwest Ohio faces a pleasant Sunday with plenty of dry \ntime and highs in the 60s, while northwest Indiana will see falling \ntemperatures in the late afternoon. \n\nHighs in the 40s for the remainder of the 7-day forecast period will \nbe rude awakening but close to normal for the final full week of \nNovember. Before Canadian high pressure settles over the Midwest, a \nmodest upper-level impulse ripples through the northwest flow \nperhaps sparking a brief period of the Lake Effect early Tuesday \nmorning. The magnitude of the cold air (-8C at 850mb) is \nappropriate, yet high pressure may quickly undercut any growing \nequilibrum-levels disrupting any emerging lake effect.\n\nLate-week remains seasonably cool with the prospects of a low \ndeveloping over the far eastern US. The magnitude of any trough \nremains in flux as noted by 500-mb height run-to-run deltas. \nPreexisting high pressure may steer any precipitation elsewhere but \nin absence of any true model consistency, we'll offer 20-30% POPs \nWednesday night through Thursday for the time being.",
